{"date": "2026-10-12", "post_id": 1481216, "title": "Jupiter and the Moons", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-12-jupiter-and-the-moons/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> How many moons can you see? The best answer is five: four orbiting <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/\">Jupiter</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/#h-how-the-moon-got-its-name\">Luna</a> orbiting Earth. This multi-moon picture was possible because <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-november-30-artemis-1-flight-day-13/\">Earth's Moon</a> moved directly <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-august-8-moon-meets-jupiter/\">in front</a> of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/\">Jupiter</a> last week. The <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeKFXPOP1y1/\">featured image</a> is a series of exposures near alignment taken from <a href=\"https://youtu.be/91YWWW10suA\">Westchester</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_(state)\">New York</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States\">USA</a>. The sunlit crescent of Earth's Moon, on the lower left, is overexposed, while the Moon's night side, on the upper right, is faintly illuminated by <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-april-3-the-da-vinci-glow/\">Earthshine</a>. Lined up <a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/aww/comments/b7s5sw/came_home_from_work_the_other_day_to_find_all_5/#lightbox\">nearly vertically</a> above our Moon, listed from bottom to top, are Jupiter's bright <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/history/410-years-ago-galileo-discovers-jupiters-moons/\">Galilean satellites</a>: Ganymede, Io, Callisto, and Europa. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-september-1-callisto-dirty-battered-iceball/\">Callisto</a>, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-november-28-ganymede-from-juno/\">Ganymede</a>, and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-january-20-io-in-true-color/\">Io</a> are larger than Earth's Moon, while <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/europa/\">Europa</a> is only slightly smaller. NASA's robotic spacecraft <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/juno/\">Juno</a> is currently orbiting <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-january-18-jupiter-from-the-webb-space-telescope/\">Jupiter</a> and has made close passes near three of these moons.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> stairway to heaven", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/chiragupreti/\">Chirag Upreti</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/chiragupreti/\">Chirag Upreti</a>", "alt": "In front of a dark background an image of Earth&#039;s Moon  appears with a lower crescent brightly lit. The rest of the Moon is also faintly visible. Above the Moon is a bright point of  light surrounded by four fainter points of light in row. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-12-jupiter-and-the-moons/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/JupiterMoons_Upreti_3105.jpg?w=0&h=0&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 12 - Jupiter and the Moons</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-12-jupiter-and-the-moons/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: How many moons can you see? The best answer is five: four orbiting Jupiter and Luna orbiting Earth. This multi-moon picture was possible because Earth&#039;s Moon moved directly in front of Jupiter last week. The featured image is a series of exposures near alignment taken from Westchester, New York, USA. The sunlit crescent of Earth&#039;s Moon, on the lower left, is overexposed, while the Moon&#039;s night side, on the upper right, is faintly illuminated by Earthshine. Lined up nearly vertically above our Moon, listed from bottom to top, are Jupiter&#039;s bright Galilean satellites: Ganymede, Io, Callisto, and Europa. Callisto, Ganymede, and Io are larger than Earth&#039;s Moon, while Europa is only slightly smaller. NASA&#039;s robotic spacecraft Juno is currently orbiting Jupiter and has made close passes near three of these moons.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: stairway to heaven\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"jupiter and the moons\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Jupiter and the Moons\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-12-jupiter-and-the-moons/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/JupiterMoons_Upreti_3105.jpg?w=0&#038;h=0&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/JupiterMoons_Upreti_3105.jpg?w=0&#038;h=0&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"In front of a dark background an image of Earth&#039;s Moon  appears with a lower crescent brightly lit. The rest of the Moon is also faintly visible. Above the Moon is a bright point of  light surrounded by four fainter points of light in row. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-12T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-10T20:55:08-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Jupiter and the Moons\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/JupiterMoons_Upreti_3105.jpg?w=0&#038;h=0&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 12<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/JupiterMoons_Upreti_3105.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/JupiterMoons_Upreti_3105.jpg\" alt=\"In front of a dark background an image of Earth&#039;s Moon  appears with a lower crescent brightly lit. The rest of the Moon is also faintly visible. Above the Moon is a bright point of  light surrounded by four fainter points of light in row. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Jupiter and the Moons </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/chiragupreti/\">Chirag Upreti</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> How many moons can you see? The best answer is five: four orbiting <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/\">Jupiter</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/#h-how-the-moon-got-its-name\">Luna</a> orbiting Earth. This multi-moon picture was possible because <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-november-30-artemis-1-flight-day-13/\">Earth's Moon</a> moved directly <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-august-8-moon-meets-jupiter/\">in front</a> of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/\">Jupiter</a> last week. The <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeKFXPOP1y1/\">featured image</a> is a series of exposures near alignment taken from <a href=\"https://youtu.be/91YWWW10suA\">Westchester</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_(state)\">New York</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States\">USA</a>. The sunlit crescent of Earth's Moon, on the lower left, is overexposed, while the Moon's night side, on the upper right, is faintly illuminated by <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-april-3-the-da-vinci-glow/\">Earthshine</a>. Lined up <a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/aww/comments/b7s5sw/came_home_from_work_the_other_day_to_find_all_5/#lightbox\">nearly vertically</a> above our Moon, listed from bottom to top, are Jupiter's bright <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/history/410-years-ago-galileo-discovers-jupiters-moons/\">Galilean satellites</a>: Ganymede, Io, Callisto, and Europa. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-september-1-callisto-dirty-battered-iceball/\">Callisto</a>, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-november-28-ganymede-from-juno/\">Ganymede</a>, and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-january-20-io-in-true-color/\">Io</a> are larger than Earth's Moon, while <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/europa/\">Europa</a> is only slightly smaller. NASA's robotic spacecraft <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/juno/\">Juno</a> is currently orbiting <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-january-18-jupiter-from-the-webb-space-telescope/\">Jupiter</a> and has made close passes near three of these moons.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> stairway to heaven\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-11-ice-fishing-for-cosmic-neutrinos/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261012/html"}